  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "If a quantity is multiplied by the same growth rate every year, which model is suitable for this type of problem?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "A geometric sequence",
    "B": "An arithmetic sequence with a common difference other than 0",
    "C": "Only use the mean",
    "D": "The slope of a linear function"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Assume the starting quantity and fixed yearly multiplier are nonzero. Every yearly quantity is then nonzero, and dividing it by the preceding year's quantity gives that multiplier. The ratio of consecutive terms is constant, so it is a geometric sequence.\n\nThe stem does not state these nonzero conditions. Starting at \\(0\\) gives \\(0,0,0,\\ldots\\); using multiplier \\(0\\) from a starting quantity of \\(1\\) gives \\(1,0,0,\\ldots\\). Both contain the undefined ratio \\(0/0\\), so neither is a geometric sequence under the supplied consecutive-ratio definition. Thus A is the intended answer under the nonzero assumptions, but it does not follow unconditionally from the literal stem.",
